wasm Linux has no return-twice fork or vfork. A clone without CLONE_VM
copies the parent's memory, so it gives ash real fork semantics as long as
the code after fork in the child is a callback rather than a second return.

forkshell gains a continuation argument. Each of its five callers (here-doc
writer, command substitution, subshell, pipeline member, external command)
moves its "pid == 0" branch into a *_child function and passes its locals in
a struct; the parent half stays inline. The child runs on a fresh 8MiB stack,
matching the main process, because subshells recurse through evaltree.

The parent's exception handlers are frames the child's stack does not have,
so the callback installs its own. A forked child would unwind to ash_main's
handler, which always calls exitshell because shlvl > 0; the callback does
the same.

